A||N||3||FieroSTETZ||AC compressor||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||06-13-2000||02:01 AM||||i was wondering- would it hurt anything to drive around with no belt to the ac compressor? right now i have no coolant in it, but that is the last thing on my list- could i safely run without the belt? also, would i free up a few horses this way? my overheating problem is narrowed down to 2 things- bad water pump or clogged radiator- wont know until i have a chance to change the water pump (in my opinion, always get the remanufactured ones, they have metal impellers!) after i change the pump if it stll overheats i think its the radiator.||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||06-13-2000||09:36 AM||||If it's a (stock) V6, it shouldn't hurt anything to run it without the A/C belt.<P>Won't free up any appreciable hp, since the A/C clutch won't (shouldn't) engage if there's no refrigerant in the system. <P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 06-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroshop||06-13-2000||10:26 AM||||By not charging the system and not running the sytem periodically you may have the ac pump sieze up. Those are expensive to replace.||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||06-13-2000||11:09 AM||||Good point.  I should have said it shouldn't hurt the ENGINE (instead of "anything") to run it w/o the A/C belt.<P>NEVER generalize! I always say that...<P>By the way, if you're trying to cure an overheating problem by process of elimination, wouldn't the next easiest thing be to remove radiator & have it rodded out?  No gasket surfaces to clean, etc..<P>I've never done the radiator, but have done front cover & timing chain, with engine still in the car (200K+ mile V6).  Left water pump attached to front cover, so I wouldn't have to scrape as many gasket surfaces.  Just lazy, I guess.    <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 06-13-2000).]||||reg||1
